# ABOUTME: Declares what `use POSIX` installs: every exported name's :export/:export_ok mark,
# ABOUTME: tags and prototype. Measured on perl 5.42.0 with POSIX 2.23.
#
# POSIX builds its lists (@EXPORT, @EXPORT_OK, %EXPORT_TAGS) inside its own
# import, measured empty (0/0/0) right after `require POSIX` and full
# (587/179/35) only once a use has run; held_to_perl.pl primes a bare use
# before reading them. A bare `use POSIX` installs 586 subs and the
# variable %SIGRT (measured): 169 of those subs are forward-declared and
# not defined until AUTOLOAD runs, so the install check asks `exists
# &X::name`, not `defined`.
#
# Every name with a `()` prototype is a constant, typed here by what perl
# returns, measured with B::svref_2object's SV flags: plain Int, or Num
# for the handful of floating-point limits and math constants. Twelve
# constants are macros this platform's libc has not defined; perl's
# AUTOLOAD stub for each dies "Your vendor has not defined POSIX macro
# ...", so each is None, never returning, as CORE.tpm types `die`. Two are
# perl's own true value (builtin::is_bool), so each is Boolean, not Int.
# perl's HUGE_VAL, INFINITY and Inf are the lattice's Inf, and NAN and NaN
# its NaN, neither a finite Num (RFC 0001, types.go: "NaN and Inf are
# members of Str, not Num").
#
# Every other exported name -- every function POSIX defines, from io.h's
# ones to the AUTOLOAD-only replacements for perl's own operators -- has
# no prototype perl can report (measured: prototype() is undef for all
# of them), so each stays a bare `sub NAME :mark;` here: the follow-on
# issue (declaring POSIX's functions) types its signature and return and
# holds it to the return-type oracle.
package POSIX;
our Hash[Str|CodeRef] %SIGRT :export(signal_h);
# ARG_MAX: perl dies calling it, "Your vendor has not defined POSIX macro ARG_MAX, used at dc07-measure-constants.pl line 13" (measured).
